","dstPath":"x-pack/plugin/esql/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/plan/logical/MvExpand.java","srcChanges":[],"dstChanges":[{"pos":3296,"endPos":3301,"startLine":105,"startLineOffset":66,"endLine":105,"endLineOffset":71,"length":6}]},{"id":"x-pack/plugin/esql/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/plan/logical/MvExpand.java-x-pack/plugin/esql/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/plan/logical/MvExpand.java--1394:1407,1513:1532","promptId":"#1NDGQ","srcPath":"x-pack/plugin/esql/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/plan/logical/MvExpand.java","dstPath":"x-pack/plugin/esql/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/plan/logical/MvExpand.java","srcChanges":[],"dstChanges":[{"pos":1394,"endPos":1407,"startLine":34,"startLineOffset":98,"endLine":34,"endLineOffset":111,"length":14},{"pos":1513,"endPos":1532,"startLine":38,"startLineOffset":8,"endLine":38,"endLineOffset":27,"length":20}]},{"id":"x-pack/plugin/esql/src/test/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/plan/logical/MvExpandSerializationTests.java-x-pack/plugin/esql/src/test/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/plan/logical/MvExpandSerializationTests.java--1154:1158","promptId":"#S4W0R","srcPath":"x-pack/plugin/esql/src/test/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/plan/logical/MvExpandSerializationTests.java","dstPath":"x-pack/plugin/esql/src/test/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/plan/logical/MvExpandSerializationTests.java","srcChanges":[],"dstChanges":[{"pos":1154,"endPos":1158,"startLine":25,"startLineOffset":61,"endLine":25,"endLineOffset":65,"length":5}]}]}],"groundTruthGeneratedCommentsNodes":[{"groundTruth":{"original_line":36,"line":36,"comment_lines":["[][36]"],"review_comment":["Interesting that this code previously did not push the limit down past the MvExpand at all, but the bug reports indicate that that was happening. Was it happening somewhere else? If so, should that location be modified?","Oh yeah, good point, I forgot about it...\r\nWe have another rule, called `DuplicateLimitAfterMvExpand`, that does exactly what I describe above: \r\n`MV_EXPAND | LIMIT` -\u003e `LIMIT | MV_EXPAND | LIMIT`\r\n\r\nI think we can remove it\r\n","There is another rule, [AddDefaultTopN](https://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/blob/main/x-pack/plugin/esql/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/optimizer/rules/logical/AddDefaultTopN.java), that is intrinsically wrong (it adds an arbitrary limit at the beginning of the query) and that IMHO should be removed as well, but I\u0027m not sure this fix is enough to do it, maybe the problems it tries to solve are not only related to MV_EXPAND."],"path":"x-pack/plugin/esql/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/optimizer/rules/logical/PushDownAndCombineLimits.java","id":34963,"created_at":"2024-10-25T10:06:05Z","original_position":4,"side":"RIGHT","position":4,"submitted_on_commit":"f471d54bc902b36f37035ea1c98754d749018368","diff_hunk":"@@ -33,6 +33,14 @@ public LogicalPlan rule(Limit limit) {\n         } else if (limit.child() instanceof UnaryPlan unary) {\n             if (unary instanceof Eval || unary instanceof Project || unary instanceof RegexExtract || unary instanceof Enrich) {\n                 return unary.replaceChild(limit.replaceChild(unary.child()));\n+            } else if (unary instanceof MvExpand mvx) {","submitted_line":36},"generatedCommentsNodes":[{"comment":"The `limit()` method is added to `MvExpand` and used in several places. However, there are no checks for null or invalid values (negative numbers) when using the limit. This could lead to unexpected behavior if a negative or null value is passed.","nodes":[{"id":"x-pack/plugin/esql/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/planner/Mapper.java-x-pack/plugin/esql/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/planner/Mapper.java-10474:10572-10638:10759,10772:10968,10988:10994","promptId":"#EMHVE","srcPath":"x-pack/plugin/esql/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/planner/Mapper.java","dstPath":"x-pack/plugin/esql/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch